  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Showers and thunderstorms return to the area during \nthe second half of the weekend and early next week.\n\nA shortwave-trough dropping from the Great Lks into the OH Valley \nover the weekend is expected to become cutoff by early next week. A \nweak area of low pressure is expected to form to its east slowly \ncrossing the area Saturday night into early Monday. Numerous showers \nand thunderstorms are anticipated Saturday night through Monday. \nShear and instability look minimal due to weak winds aloft and \nwidespread cloudiness, so severe threat appears very low. Rainfall \namounts of half to one inch spaced over a long period of time mean \nthis rainfall would be largely beneficial and should not cause any \nflooding concerns. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Minimal chances of rain through Friday night. \n\nThe Mid Atlantic will be in between high pressure approaching \nfrom the upper Great Lakes and low pressure moving into New \nEngland through Thursday. This pressure gradient will result in\ngusty northwest winds through Thursday, and moisture from the \nGreat Lakes will result in a robust cumulus/stratocumulus field.\nTemperatures will be slightly below normal along with lower \nhumidity.\n\nA couple of sprinkles or perhaps a shower or two could rotate\ninto the northcentral and northeastern parts of MD overnight\nfrom the low pressure system well to our north. The forecast \nfor Thursday remains dry for now, but there is some consensus in\nanother shortwave spoke dropping southward during the \nafternoon, potentially bringing more isolated showers to NE MD.\n\nThe surface high will reach the area Friday. Shortwave ridging\nwill approach from the west as the upper low departs. Dry\nconditions are expected with high temperatures closer to\nclimatology in the mid 80s to near 90 and lows in the 60s to\naround 70.",
